<body>
  <Header />
  <main>
  <article class="container">
    <header class="article-header">
      <span class="tag">Writing Assistants</span>
      <h1>Notion AI vs Grammarly: Which Writing Assistant Is Better in 2026?</h1>
      <p class="meta">Updated July 2026</p>
    </header>

    <p class="lead">
      Notion AI and Grammarly are the two writing assistants most often shortlisted by knowledge workers in 2026, and they take opposite approaches. Notion AI wins on content generation, summarization, and workspace context inside Notion. Grammarly wins on grammar checking, tone analysis, and real-time editing across every app you write in. This comparison breaks down content generation, grammar checking, pricing, integrations, and use cases so you can pick the right one for your workflow.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-top">
      <a href="https://www.notion.so" class="btn btn-primary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try Notion AI</a>
      <a href="https://www.grammarly.com" class="btn btn-secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try Grammarly</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Quick Comparison</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Notion AI</th><th>Grammarly</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Best For</td><td>Content generation, summarization, Notion users</td><td>Grammar, tone, clarity, cross-app editing</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Core Capability</td><td>Generative AI for drafts and summaries</td><td>Real-time grammar and style checking</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Free Tier</td><td>Limited AI responses in Notion free</td><td>Grammar and spelling checks forever</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Paid Plan</td><td>$10/user/mo (AI add-on)</td><td>$12/mo Premium (annual billing)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Content Generation</td><td>Strong, with workspace context</td><td>Limited via GrammarlyGO</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Grammar Checking</td><td>Basic, on-demand</td><td>Best in class, real-time</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tone Analysis</td><td>Limited</td><td>Advanced, with tone detector</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Plagiarism Check</td><td>No</td><td>Yes, 16 billion web pages (Premium)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Workspace Context</td><td>Yes, references your Notion docs</td><td>No, works on current text only</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Integrations</td><td>Notion only</td><td>Browser, Office, Gmail, Slack, mobile</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Team Collaboration</td><td>Built into Notion</td><td>Business and Enterprise plans</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mobile Keyboard</td><td>No</td><td>Yes, iOS and Android</td></tr>
        <tr><td>API</td><td>Yes, via Notion API</td><td>Yes, via Grammarly Text Editor SDK</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Languages</td><td>Multiple, including translation</td><td>English primary, beta for others</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What Is Notion AI?</h2>
    <p>Notion AI is the artificial intelligence layer built into Notion, the all-in-one workspace founded in 2013 by Ivan Zhao and Simon Last. Notion launched its AI features in early 2023, and by 2026 they have become a core reason teams adopt the platform. Notion AI is not a standalone tool: it lives inside Notion documents, databases, and wikis, which means it can reference your existing content when generating new text.</p>
    <p>The current version can generate first drafts, summarize long documents, extract action items from meeting notes, translate text between languages, rewrite sentences for clarity, and answer questions about your workspace content. The defining feature is workspace context: ask Notion AI to write a project brief, and it can pull details from your Q3 roadmap, product specs, and team wiki without you leaving the page. Pricing is $10 per user per month as an add-on to any Notion plan, including the free plan, with a limited number of free AI responses for testing.</p>

    <h2>What Is Grammarly?</h2>
    <p>Grammarly is a writing assistant founded in 2009 by Max Lytvyn, Alex Shevchenko, and Dmytro Lider. The company has spent over a decade perfecting one thing: making writing clear, correct, and compelling. Grammarly is used by over 30 million people daily and is the most widely deployed writing assistant in the world. Unlike Notion AI, which generates content, Grammarly acts as a real-time editor that follows you across every app where you write.</p>
    <p>The current version works through a browser extension, desktop app, mobile keyboard, Microsoft Office plugin, and Google Docs integration. Grammarly checks grammar, spelling, punctuation, clarity, tone, and engagement in real time, underlining issues as you type. The Premium plan adds vocabulary enhancement, plagiarism detection against 16 billion web pages, and advanced tone analysis. GrammarlyGO, introduced in 2023, adds generative AI for rewriting and ideation, though it is weaker than Notion AI for long-form drafting. Pricing starts with a permanent free tier, Premium at $12 per month billed annually, Business at $15 per user per month, and Enterprise for large organizations.</p>

    <h2>Content Generation</h2>
    <p>Content generation is where Notion AI clearly wins. Notion AI can draft blog posts, emails, meeting summaries, and project briefs from a single prompt, and it can reference your workspace content for context. Grammarly can rewrite and ideate through GrammarlyGO, but it is not designed for long-form drafting.</p>

    <h3>Notion AI Generation</h3>
    <p>Notion AI excels at generating first drafts and summaries. You can ask it to write a blog post outline from a sentence, summarize a 5,000-word research document into bullet points, extract action items from meeting notes, or translate a doc into Spanish. The workspace context is the standout feature: ask it to draft a project brief using details from your Q3 roadmap, and it pulls real data from your Notion databases. For teams that use Notion as their knowledge hub, this contextual awareness is unmatched.</p>

    <h3>Grammarly Generation</h3>
    <p>GrammarlyGO can generate text, rewrite sentences, and ideate, but it is built for editing rather than drafting. It works inside the Grammarly editor and browser extension, offering suggestions like "make this formal" or "shorten this paragraph." GrammarlyGO is useful for refining existing text, but it cannot reference your company wiki or project docs the way Notion AI can. For long-form content generation from scratch, Grammarly is weaker.</p>

    <h3>Winner: Notion AI</h3>
    <p>For content generation, summarization, and workspace-aware drafting, Notion AI wins clearly. GrammarlyGO is a useful refinement tool, but Notion AI is a genuine generative assistant.</p>

    <h2>Grammar and Style Checking</h2>
    <p>Grammar and style checking is where Grammarly clearly wins. Grammarly provides real-time, surface-level correction across every app, while Notion AI offers basic on-demand fixes inside Notion only.</p>

    <h3>Grammarly Checking</h3>
    <p>Grammarly is the best grammar checker in the category. It underlines errors as you type in Gmail, Google Docs, Word, Slack, Twitter, LinkedIn, and any other web text field. The free tier catches spelling, grammar, and basic punctuation. Premium adds clarity suggestions, vocabulary enhancement, passive voice detection, tone analysis, and plagiarism checking. The tone detector is especially valuable for non-native English speakers, who can see whether their writing sounds formal, casual, confident, or friendly before sending.</p>

    <h3>Notion AI Checking</h3>
    <p>Notion AI can fix grammar and spelling through its "improve writing" feature, but it is basic. You select text, click "improve writing," and Notion AI rewrites it for clarity. There are no real-time underlines, no tone analysis, and no plagiarism detection. For quick fixes inside Notion documents, it works. For serious editing across multiple apps, it does not compete with Grammarly.</p>

    <h3>Winner: Grammarly</h3>
    <p>For grammar, style, and tone checking, Grammarly wins by a wide margin. Real-time suggestions across every app, advanced tone analysis, and plagiarism detection are features Notion AI cannot match.</p>

    <h2>Integrations and Availability</h2>
    <p>Integrations are where Grammarly dominates. Grammarly works everywhere you write, while Notion AI works only inside Notion.</p>

    <h3>Grammarly Availability</h3>
    <p>Grammarly is available as a browser extension for Chrome, Firefox, Edge, and Safari, a desktop app for macOS and Windows, a mobile keyboard for iOS and Android, a Microsoft Office plugin, a Google Docs add-on, and a Slack integration. This means Grammarly follows you across email, social media, documents, and messaging, providing consistent correction everywhere. The Grammarly Text Editor SDK also lets developers embed grammar checking into their own apps.</p>

    <h3>Notion AI Availability</h3>
    <p>Notion AI works only inside Notion. If you write in Google Docs, Word, Gmail, or Slack, Notion AI cannot help you. This is a significant limitation for anyone who writes across multiple apps. The trade-off is that inside Notion, Notion AI has deep context that Grammarly cannot access, but for cross-app writers, Grammarly is the clear choice.</p>

    <h3>Winner: Grammarly</h3>
    <p>For availability across apps and platforms, Grammarly wins. Notion AI is powerful inside Notion, but it does not follow you outside the workspace.</p>

    <h2>Pricing Comparison</h2>
    <p>Pricing is close, and the value calculation depends on what you need. Notion AI is cheaper per month, but Grammarly offers a permanent free tier that covers basic grammar checking.</p>

    <h3>Notion AI Pricing</h3>
    <p>Notion AI costs $10 per user per month as an add-on to any Notion plan, including the free plan. Every Notion workspace gets a limited number of free AI responses for testing. Notion Plus is $10 per user per month, Business is $18, and Enterprise is custom-priced. For a small team of 5 people on Notion Plus with AI, the total is $100 per month, which is reasonable for a unified workspace with AI built in.</p>

    <h3>Grammarly Pricing</h3>
    <p>Grammarly Free is permanent and covers grammar, spelling, and basic punctuation. Grammarly Premium is $12 per month billed annually, $20 per month billed monthly, or $144 per year. Premium adds clarity, tone, vocabulary, and plagiarism checks. Grammarly Business is $15 per user per month with style guides and brand tones. For individuals, the annual Premium plan at $144 per year is the best value, effectively $12 per month.</p>

    <h3>Winner: Depends</h3>
    <p>If you only need basic grammar checking, Grammarly Free is the cheapest option at zero cost. If you need content generation and already use Notion, Notion AI at $10 per month offers more value. For professionals who need advanced grammar and tone analysis, Grammarly Premium at $12 per month is worth the premium.</p>

    <h2>Pros and Cons</h2>

    <div class="pros-box">
      <h3>Notion AI Pros</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Best content generation in the category, with workspace context</li>
        <li>Can summarize long documents and extract action items</li>
        <li>References your Notion databases and docs for contextual drafts</li>
        <li>Integrated into a unified workspace with tasks, notes, and wikis</li>
        <li>Translation across multiple languages</li>
        <li>Reasonable pricing at $10 per user per month</li>
        <li>Strong for team collaboration inside Notion</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="cons-box">
      <h3>Notion AI Cons</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Works only inside Notion, no cross-app support</li>
        <li>Grammar checking is basic, no real-time suggestions</li>
        <li>No tone analysis or plagiarism detection</li>
        <li>Requires a Notion account and workspace to use</li>
        <li>Generated content can be generic without careful prompting</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="pros-box">
      <h3>Grammarly Pros</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Best grammar and style checking in the category</li>
        <li>Works across every app via browser extension and plugins</li>
        <li>Real-time suggestions as you type</li>
        <li>Advanced tone analysis for confident communication</li>
        <li>Plagiarism detection against 16 billion web pages</li>
        <li>Permanent free tier for basic grammar checking</li>
        <li>Mobile keyboard for iOS and Android</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="cons-box">
      <h3>Grammarly Cons</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Cannot generate long-form content from scratch</li>
        <li>No workspace context, works on current text only</li>
        <li>Premium is more expensive at $12 to $20 per month</li>
        <li>Occasionally over-suggests and strips personality</li>
        <li>GrammarlyGO is weaker than Notion AI for drafting</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Who Should Use Which?</h2>

    <h3>Choose Notion AI if you</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Already use Notion as your primary workspace</li>
      <li>Need to generate first drafts, summaries, and outlines</li>
      <li>Want AI that references your company wiki and project docs</li>
      <li>Work in a team that collaborates inside Notion</li>
      <li>Translate documents between languages</li>
      <li>Prefer an all-in-one workspace over separate tools</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Choose Grammarly if you</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Write across email, docs, social media, and messaging</li>
      <li>Need real-time grammar and spelling correction</li>
      <li>Want tone analysis for professional communication</li>
      <li>Are a non-native English speaker seeking writing confidence</li>
      <li>Need plagiarism detection for academic or journalistic work</li>
      <li>Want a free tier that covers basic grammar checking</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Real-World Use Cases</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Content Team Drafting Blog Posts</h3>
      <p>A content team uses Notion as their editorial workspace. Writers use Notion AI to generate outlines from keywords, draft first versions of posts, and summarize research notes into briefs. Because Notion AI references the team's style guide and brand voice docs, drafts are on-brand from the first pass. The team then runs final drafts through Grammarly Premium for tone and clarity polish before publishing. The combined workflow costs $22 per writer per month and cuts drafting time roughly in half.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Sales Rep Writing Cold Emails</h3>
      <p>A sales rep writes cold emails in Gmail throughout the day. The rep uses Grammarly's browser extension to catch typos, check tone, and ensure emails sound confident but not aggressive. Notion AI is not useful here because the rep writes in Gmail, not Notion. Grammarly Premium's tone detector is the deciding feature, helping the rep adjust formality based on the prospect's industry.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Project Manager Summarizing Meetings</h3>
      <p>A project manager stores meeting notes in Notion. After each meeting, the manager uses Notion AI to summarize the notes into bullet points, extract action items, and assign tasks to attendees. The AI references the project database to link action items to existing tasks. Grammarly is not involved in this workflow because the deliverable is a summary, not polished prose. Notion AI's workspace context is the key feature.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Non-Native English Speaker Writing Reports</h3>
      <p>A non-native English speaker writes reports in Google Docs and emails in Gmail. The user relies on Grammarly Premium for real-time grammar correction, tone analysis, and vocabulary suggestions. The tone detector helps the user understand whether a report sounds formal enough for leadership. Notion AI is not useful here because the user does not work in Notion. Grammarly's cross-app availability and tone analysis are the deciding factors.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Student Writing Academic Papers</h3>
      <p>A university student writes research papers in Word and Google Docs. The student uses Grammarly Premium for grammar, clarity, and plagiarism detection before submission. The plagiarism checker, which scans 16 billion web pages, is essential for academic integrity. Notion AI is not suitable for this workflow because it cannot check plagiarism and does not integrate with Word or Google Docs. Grammarly is the clear choice for academic writing.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Final Verdict</h2>
    <div class="verdict">
      <p>Notion AI and Grammarly are both excellent, but they serve different needs. Notion AI is the generator: it drafts content, summarizes documents, and references your workspace for context. Grammarly is the editor: it checks grammar, analyzes tone, and polishes your writing across every app.</p>
      <p>For teams that live in Notion and need content generation, Notion AI is the better choice. The workspace context is unmatched, and the pricing is reasonable. For professionals who write across email, docs, and social media and need polished output, Grammarly is the better choice. The real-time checking and tone analysis are features Notion AI cannot match.</p>
      <p>Many writers use both: Notion AI to draft and summarize inside Notion, Grammarly to polish and check across every app. The combined $22 per month is less than a single hour of professional editing, and the workflow covers generation to publication.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cta-bottom">
      <h3>Pick your writing assistant</h3>
      <p>Start with the tool that matches your writing workflow.</p>
      <div class="cta-top" style="justify-content: center; margin-bottom: 0;">
